      <p>The Unified Identification Number is a six-digit number automatically assigned to anyone who enters the UAE. This six-digit number is used to identify a person entering the UAE for any reason (employment, tourist, dependent, etc.) for immigration records. This number will also be used for residence visa applications and Emirates ID processing.</p>
      <p>The Unified Identification Number is a six-digit number automatically assigned to anyone who enters the UAE. This number is used to identify a person entering the UAE for any reason (employment, tourism, dependency, etc.) for immigration records. It is also used for residence visa applications and Emirates ID processing.</p>
      <p>Why do UID numbers need merging?</p>
      <p>In some cases, individuals are assigned multiple UID numbers due to system errors. Each individual must only have one UID number. Duplicate records in the immigration system can cause problems when entering or exiting the UAE and applying for a residency visa. To reconcile immigration records, multiple UID numbers must be merged. Merging UID numbers requires submitting the original passport to Immigration along with the UID numbers to be merged.</p>
      <p>To reconcile immigration records, Multiple UID numbers have to be merged in order for immigration records to be reconciled. Merging UID numbers requires submitting the original passport to Immigration with the UID numbers to be merged.</p>
      <div class="section-head">Requirement(s)</div>
      <ol>
        <li>Passport copy including signature page</li>
        <li>Old and current visa copies</li>
        <li>Cancellation paper If recently had a cancelled visa</li>
      </ol>
